WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:37.200
We've had written history for 5000 years.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:41.820
People have been generally literate for less
than 500 years.

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:47.570
But we’ve had culture for 150,000 years.

00:00:47.570 --> 00:00:55.150
Identifiable culture for at least 25,000 so
the vast period of our enculturation was preliterate

00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:59.610
enculturation before we could write down the
rules and transmit them.

00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:05.940
How were they transmitted assuming we had
culture well there's a variety of mechanisms.

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:11.220
we have a tendency to see elements of the
world in personified form because most of

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:15.860
the inter relationships we have with the world
are actually social or interpersonal relationships

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:20.300
so that’s basically how our brain is structured
and as we evolved we developed the capacity

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:25.680
to extend our cognitive ability beyond the
merely social to take in the world as such

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:29.110
but the categories that we use to do that
was still fundamentally social.

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:35.470
Now the reason the unknown per se is symbolized
as feminine is because the critical feature

00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:40.100
of the feminine and I don’t mean the female
individual I mean the feminine category is

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:43.030
its capacity to generate new forms.

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:49.280
And the unknown is such is is logically and
appropriately symbolized by the feminine because

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:55.200
it’s the bringer forth of all things which
is to say that the background of existence

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:59.780
the unknowable background of existence is
the thing that generates everything.

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:04.640
paired with that of course according to the
schema that we've been working with is the

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:06.370
archetype of the great father.

00:02:06.370 --> 00:02:11.010
and the archetype of the great father is the
archetype of tradition fundamentally.

00:02:11.010 --> 00:02:16.770
The great weight of the past
because as you incorporate your past the past

00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:22.120
of your culture through the intermediation
of your parents you learn routines and rituals

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:26.790
for structure in the unknown now those routines
and rituals are patterns of action that you

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:29.950
use in the world as such.

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:34.950
And also a complex patterns of action that
you use to structure your own interpretations

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:39.030
and your own motor output and your own conceptualizations
when you're dealing with other people and

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:44.890
so that would be the absorption the most fundamentally
of your cultural rules and the fact of those

00:02:44.890 --> 00:02:51.930
cultural rules and their incarnation in you
essentially keeps chaos at bay which means

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:56.590
if two of you share the same cultural structure,
assuming you're playing the same game so to

00:02:56.590 --> 00:03:00.680
speak that means that you can each predict
each other’s responses which is very, very

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:05.480
useful because that way you can be sure that
you can trust the other person you can more

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:09.730
or less infer their goals because after all
their value structure’s similar to yours

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:13.870
and if you can infer their goals that means
you can embody their emotions,

00:03:13.870 --> 00:03:17.959
So that’s the benevolent aspect of tradition
right the part that protects and shelters

00:03:17.959 --> 00:03:22.930
you and structures the nature of your being
in the face of existential terror and doubt

00:03:22.930 --> 00:03:29.130
so to speak but there's also an aspect of
tradition that’s terrible, tyrannical.

00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:34.280
it’s the part that marches young men off
to war say in defense of the structures that

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:35.280
it’s protecting.

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:40.239
It’s the part that says when you're a teenager
wear this and not this or you'll be the target

00:03:40.239 --> 00:03:41.989
of mockery for all your peers.

00:03:41.989 --> 00:03:47.630
It’s the part of the structure that crushes
the creative life out of you because the tyrannical

00:03:47.630 --> 00:03:52.680
aspect of social order doesn’t want your
creative life so to speak what it wants is

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:59.019
your obedience because your obedience is what
makes the machine run smooth and so you're

00:03:59.019 --> 00:04:04.740
always in an ambivalent relationship with
regards to security to authority on the one

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:09.519
hand it provides you shelter and what you
need and allows you to gain the benefits of

00:04:09.519 --> 00:04:13.050
literally thousands and thousands of years
of cultural evolution.

00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:17.709
On the other hand it’s the thing that makes
you obey or face the painful consequences

00:04:17.709 --> 00:04:24.440
thereof which can range from mere social exclusion
and consequent reexposure to the unknown to

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:32.590
truly oppressive practices designed to make
you be exactly like everyone else or else.

00:04:32.590 --> 00:04:38.740
And so you could say most particularly again
that that’s a standard existential problem

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:43.409
it’s a problem that’s faced by people
in every place and in ever culture, balancing

00:04:43.409 --> 00:04:50.969
the appropriate attitude towards culture

00:04:50.969 --> 00:04:56.180
So the most fundamental representation of
culture can be portrayed essentially in this

00:04:56.180 --> 00:04:58.180
manner I think.

00:04:58.180 --> 00:05:03.060
And what you see here is the dragon of chaos
of course lurking in the background and what

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:08.970
that means is that all forms come from the
formless and that the father itself is a primary

00:05:08.970 --> 00:05:14.490
form a representation of God the father and
God the father in the Christian trinity is

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:21.350
the representation of the positive and the
security and the tyrannical aspect of social

00:05:21.350 --> 00:05:26.140
order right God has a set of rules for you,
you bloody well better listen to those rules

00:05:26.140 --> 00:05:29.760
if you don’t all hell’s gonna break loose
and you know that’s a pretty reasonable

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:32.260
summary of how things work unfortunately.

00:05:32.260 --> 00:05:37.080
so on the one hand God offers security on
the other hand he offers tyranny and in total

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:43.000
that basically represents order so you can
see this representation is quite useful; shows

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:49.969
God’s standing over this city which of course
is a city committed to him fundamentally so

00:05:49.969 --> 00:05:54.260
it operates under the moral principals that
he represents and behind him is a representation

00:05:54.260 --> 00:06:01.030
of the sun which is partly a representation
of the source of all life partly a representation

00:06:01.030 --> 00:06:04.820
of the source of consciousness and illumination
right because you're conscious during the

00:06:04.820 --> 00:06:11.779
day and partly a halo representing a sort
of transcendent nature of the of the social

00:06:11.779 --> 00:06:17.770
order that structures existence so it’s
a primary phenomenon and it’s only to say

00:06:17.770 --> 00:06:23.940
that in all human experience there's a cultural
aspect and a natural aspect and a funny thing

00:06:23.940 --> 00:06:28.120
is the cultural aspect in some ways is as
natural as the natural aspect right because

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:33.430
we’re social beings we can’t exist without
society, society’s structures as our very

00:06:33.430 --> 00:06:34.930
nature.

00:06:34.930 --> 00:06:42.560
And we’re beneficiaries and victims.

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:50.669
And so then just as the case - just as is
the case with the feminine there's a two aspects

00:06:50.669 --> 00:06:55.669
that can be represented metaphorically with
regards to the masculine you can say well

00:06:55.669 --> 00:07:01.030
the secure aspect of social order is the wise
king and you can see it written in medieval

00:07:01.030 --> 00:07:05.250
representation of him here sitting on his
throne calmly in a relatively open posture

00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:10.089
that means he’s ready to listen to supplicants
to people who are coming to talk to him.

00:07:10.089 --> 00:07:14.020
He’s holding an orb with a cross on top
of it which means essentially that he’s

00:07:14.020 --> 00:07:17.979
in control of the world and that the world
is subordinated to something else that’s

00:07:17.979 --> 00:07:19.130
represented by the cross.

00:07:19.130 --> 00:07:25.680
A wise and just ruler and then his mirror
image here is the son devouring king a very

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:31.610
common mythological theme the father who wants
to destroy his son and shades of the Oedipal

00:07:31.610 --> 00:07:35.560
conflict in that of course if you remember
your Freud but basically what it means is

00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:43.969
this is that despite the fact that every human
being is an offspring of culture by nature,

00:07:43.969 --> 00:07:47.839
every human being is also in the terrible
position of facing the fact that their very

00:07:47.839 --> 00:07:52.760
individuality is likely to be crushed out
of them during during the socialization process.

00:07:52.760 --> 00:07:57.440
And in a sense that’s really not an that’s
really not avoidable I mean if you're subject

00:07:57.440 --> 00:08:02.950
to really tyrannical socialization it’s
obviously a much more cardinal problem right

00:08:02.950 --> 00:08:07.659
but even if you're subject to socialization
under normal circumstances you are still what

00:08:07.659 --> 00:08:13.150
you are rather than the many fold things that
you could've otherwise been and just to give

00:08:13.150 --> 00:08:18.960
you some sense of how dramatic a process this
really is one of the things you should know

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:25.490
is that you actually die into your brain so
one of the things you might wonder is why

00:08:25.490 --> 00:08:29.909
is it that death evolved it doesn’t really
make up much sense from a Darwinian perspective,

00:08:29.909 --> 00:08:33.729
right because you'd make the presupposition
that if you could just stick around and father

00:08:33.729 --> 00:08:38.360
children say for 250 years you'd be doing
a lot better job than the poor sap who only

00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:44.090
lived to be 30 so why is it that you only
live to be 70 and really your period of fertility

00:08:44.090 --> 00:08:46.220
is over say by the time you're 40.

00:08:46.220 --> 00:08:49.580
Why would that be what's the utility of death?

00:08:49.580 --> 00:08:54.540
And then you remember well the environment’s
always changing right in this chaotic manner

00:08:54.540 --> 00:08:57.250
that’s represented by the great mother.

00:08:57.250 --> 00:09:03.470
Can you change with it and in answer to that
is yes but only to a certain point.

00:09:03.470 --> 00:09:08.630
Which is why as people age they tend to become
more and more alienated from the current culture

00:09:08.630 --> 00:09:14.279
right they’ve they’ve adopted their position
of being say which is a more or less fixed

00:09:14.279 --> 00:09:19.240
by the time they're 25 or so once their pre
frontal cortex matures and then after that

00:09:19.240 --> 00:09:23.430
the world gets away from them they don’t
have enough biological resources left to constantly

00:09:23.430 --> 00:09:29.069
undergo new revolutionary neurological processes
and part of the reason is this.

00:09:29.069 --> 00:09:35.129
You have more neuro connections in your brain
when you're first born than you do for the

00:09:35.129 --> 00:09:39.649
rest of your life, any other time in your
life and as you learn when you're an infant

00:09:39.649 --> 00:09:44.120
and as you learn say over the first two years
what happens is that there's a plentitude

00:09:44.120 --> 00:09:48.990
of circuits and they die off leaving only
those circuits that have a function and you

00:09:48.990 --> 00:09:51.860
think about that it’s kind of a quasi Darwinian
process.

00:09:51.860 --> 00:09:56.470
And so what that means is that as you mature
and become fixed in your form you know to

00:09:56.470 --> 00:10:02.959
adopt your personality whatever it becomes,
what's happening is that the excess possibility

00:10:02.959 --> 00:10:07.529
in some sense is being demolished by experience.

00:10:07.529 --> 00:10:12.670
So the tyrannical aspect of enculturation
is something that’s real because it makes

00:10:12.670 --> 00:10:15.600
you in large part what you are.

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:22.040
And you have to understand as well that that’s
necessary because it’s better to be something,

00:10:22.040 --> 00:10:25.769
in the final analysis it’s better to be
something than to be nothing.

00:10:25.769 --> 00:10:31.709
But you know we still have residual dreams
like those expressed by Peter Pan say who's

00:10:31.709 --> 00:10:36.120
the boy that never wants to grow up because
he doesn’t wanna attain any final and fixed

00:10:36.120 --> 00:10:41.410
form and it’s interesting because in one
respect as you progress through your life

00:10:41.410 --> 00:10:46.209
you're climbing assuming things are going
well you're climbing to ever new heights but

00:10:46.209 --> 00:10:51.860
on the other hand the direction that you're
going in constantly narrows as you age so

00:10:51.860 --> 00:10:56.550
there's a real trade off there and I think
the existential angst that’s caused as a

00:10:56.550 --> 00:10:59.689
consequence of that trade off is often real.

00:10:59.689 --> 00:11:03.861
And I also think that adolescents and early
adults feel this most intently which is part

00:11:03.861 --> 00:11:09.040
of the reason why they tend to rebel against
social structures in general you know whether

00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:13.350
it’s the military industrial complex or
the corporate world or globalization or what

00:11:13.350 --> 00:11:18.920
have you is that you have these large structures
that represent the tyrannical aspect of social

00:11:18.920 --> 00:11:25.790
being and it’s no wonder that the fact of
those structures engenders rebellion, it should.

00:11:25.790 --> 00:11:29.160
On the other hand it’s also no wonder that
structures like that exist because if they

00:11:29.160 --> 00:11:35.050
didn't exist then people would have no way
of interrelating their social being and we

00:11:35.050 --> 00:11:39.470
would revert back to the sort of Hobbsian
state of war where everybody’s arms are

00:11:39.470 --> 00:11:41.790
around everybody else’s throat.

00:11:41.790 --> 00:11:44.649
That doesn’t mean the payoff is always good.

00:11:44.649 --> 00:11:50.490
So… the Freudians of course had a real field
day with this, and we’re most fundamentally

00:11:50.490 --> 00:11:52.589
concerned by it.

00:11:52.589 --> 00:11:56.709
part of the reason that Freudian psychology
has had such an immense impact on western

00:11:56.709 --> 00:12:01.910
and world culture is because Freud came along
just when classical Judeo-Christian mythological

00:12:01.910 --> 00:12:07.040
structures were on the on a serious decline
in the west and Freud stepped in with a secularized

00:12:07.040 --> 00:12:14.019
mythological version of reality which said
well there's nature and that’s the Id right

00:12:14.019 --> 00:12:18.819
that’s the wild and untamed impulses that
spring up from the animal mind and there's

00:12:18.819 --> 00:12:25.350
the ego which is the individual whose in many
ways a pawn of these id like forces and then

00:12:25.350 --> 00:12:31.860
on top of the ego crushing it down into the
id so to speak is the superego which is the

00:12:31.860 --> 00:12:37.230
internal and external embodiment of social
order and morality so you can see the mythological

00:12:37.230 --> 00:12:39.269
substructure underlying Freud.

00:12:39.269 --> 00:12:43.810
Freud said well ego is always being shaped
by the superego and always compulsion it’s

00:12:43.810 --> 00:12:44.810
“don’t, don’t”.

00:12:44.810 --> 00:12:50.769
It’s always “no” it’s like Old Testament
morality and the incarnation of the ten commandments.

00:12:50.769 --> 00:12:54.410
Whatever you wanna do if it feels good the
probability is high that it’s immoral from

00:12:54.410 --> 00:12:55.970
the perspective of the social world

00:12:55.970 --> 00:13:00.290
and you can really see this with children
you know it’s really remarkable to watch

00:13:00.290 --> 00:13:05.430
them because my sense is and I don’t think
this is just because I'm a particularly tyrannical

00:13:05.430 --> 00:13:10.540
father is that children often get in more
trouble for having fun than they do for any

00:13:10.540 --> 00:13:16.149
other reason because their capacity for unbridled
enjoyment is so unbridled that it actually

00:13:16.149 --> 00:13:21.029
poses a threat to orderly structure so you
know if a child really gets in an active mood

00:13:21.029 --> 00:13:25.920
and is playing a very active game I mean especially
if they're somewhere between say 3 and 5 they

00:13:25.920 --> 00:13:29.970
can tear your house to shreds and no time
no time flat and you're always bawling them

00:13:29.970 --> 00:13:33.319
out like no, no, no quiet down don’t do
that.

00:13:33.319 --> 00:13:36.640
And you know they're smiling away and they're
happier than any adult you're ever gonna see

00:13:36.640 --> 00:13:40.689
in your entire life and you’re doing everything
you can to push them down so that they're

00:13:40.689 --> 00:13:44.560
they can sit quietly and read a book or whatever
it is you think they should be doing and that’s

00:13:44.560 --> 00:13:48.899
really nasty and horrible but by the same
token it’s absolutely necessary because

00:13:48.899 --> 00:13:53.279
if they don’t learn to bring their impulses
even their impulses their ludic impulses right

00:13:53.279 --> 00:13:57.959
their impulses to play under control then
nobody else can stand them and if they don’t

00:13:57.959 --> 00:14:02.720
get access to the resources there in the social
world man they have one dismal life laid out

00:14:02.720 --> 00:14:07.910
in front of them so anyways so that’s an
early description of the genesis of the conflict

00:14:07.910 --> 00:14:11.439
say between the ego and the superego and I
just wanted to point that out because it’s

00:14:11.439 --> 00:14:16.850
not just sex and aggression that gets regulated
right we can understand why that might happen

00:14:16.850 --> 00:14:20.600
but it’s also playfulness and creativity
and spontaneity and all the things that we

00:14:20.600 --> 00:14:26.110
associated with the joy of being that the
terrible tyrannical social structure puts

00:14:26.110 --> 00:14:27.610
a clamp on.

00:14:27.610 --> 00:14:33.149
So Jaak Panksepp for example has recently
demonstrated that children, boys because it’s

00:14:33.149 --> 00:14:38.709
usually boys who have attention deficit disorder
which is generally diagnosed in the school

00:14:38.709 --> 00:14:44.399
room do much better if they're placed on methylphenidate
which is a kind of amphetamine fundamentally

00:14:44.399 --> 00:14:49.069
but normal kids do better on amphetamines
too by the way they can focus better they

00:14:49.069 --> 00:14:53.740
can focus more and they can pay attention
more mostly what methylphenidate does is suppress

00:14:53.740 --> 00:14:59.680
play so Panksepp notes is that well what’s
happening with these ADHD kids hyperactive

00:14:59.680 --> 00:15:06.519
kids is they're more playful more boisterously
playful which is tends to be a masculine attribute

00:15:06.519 --> 00:15:11.559
in juvenile forms of many mammals they're
boisterously playful give them a little methylphenidate

00:15:11.559 --> 00:15:16.350
that shuts down their play behaviour and they
can sit down and focus you know and well you

00:15:16.350 --> 00:15:20.629
can understand how even if you might think
that’s necessary because apparently many

00:15:20.629 --> 00:15:25.480
people do you can also still understand that
by the same token that probably represents

00:15:25.480 --> 00:15:31.759
some kind of loss because we like to see kids
play and it’s good for them besides, so

00:15:31.759 --> 00:15:36.269
anyways Freud says the ego pops up it’s
all thrilled to death with the world a superego

00:15:36.269 --> 00:15:40.579
comes along and shuts it down and that’s
a fate that befalls all of us and not only

00:15:40.579 --> 00:15:44.740
that because Freud’s a pretty wise man all
things considered he doesn’t say that’s

00:15:44.740 --> 00:15:53.990
all to the bad he says that’s the price
we pay for social being and fair enough.

00:15:53.990 --> 00:15:58.420
you see this happening in children all the
time where part of what they do as they mature

00:15:58.420 --> 00:16:02.700
is to adopt roles so they’ll play being
a father or being a mother say and what they're

00:16:02.700 --> 00:16:09.199
doing is pulling in what they see as a as
the world view that characterizes parenthood

00:16:09.199 --> 00:16:13.790
embodying the father playing out the role
and trying to organize their motivational

00:16:13.790 --> 00:16:18.959
structures within observed framework say that
the father provides and that’s an introjections

00:16:18.959 --> 00:16:24.860
of social wisdom if you can imagine that the
role of father say has a structure like one

00:16:24.860 --> 00:16:30.350
structure would be well if you’re a father
and you're around to model then you have to

00:16:30.350 --> 00:16:33.999
be taking care of the children or at least
you have to be there often enough so that

00:16:33.999 --> 00:16:39.230
you can be a target of modeling and so you
can imagine that the spirit of the father

00:16:39.230 --> 00:16:44.709
that’s modeled at least in the optimal circumstances
is one that deals out rules and order but

00:16:44.709 --> 00:16:50.449
that also provides nurturing care and support
and that’s a kind of story we know fathers

00:16:50.449 --> 00:16:54.970
who aren't like that and maybe we know fathers
who are too much like that but all things

00:16:54.970 --> 00:17:00.999
considered on the average you have a father
role and children attempt to interject that

00:17:00.999 --> 00:17:04.510
and that’s part of the way that they learn
to modulate their own motivational resources

00:17:04.510 --> 00:17:10.809
so for example if the child observes the father
and the mother sharing which means taking

00:17:10.809 --> 00:17:16.189
each others motivational states into account
then they can act out the game of sharing

00:17:16.189 --> 00:17:21.199
with a doll say representing a child and what
they're doing is trying to imagine what it’s

00:17:21.199 --> 00:17:26.640
like inside that doll’s head treating it
like a person, by embodying the potential

00:17:26.640 --> 00:17:31.530
motivations and emotions states of that pretend
object in their own body, and then by trying

00:17:31.530 --> 00:17:36.720
to organize a high order structure which would
be like the tea party where tea is shared

00:17:36.720 --> 00:17:40.730
a higher order structure where you have a
turn because you want a turn because you're

00:17:40.730 --> 00:17:45.049
thirsty like me and then I have a turn and
we both get what we want and we can exchange

00:17:45.049 --> 00:17:46.049
information

00:17:46.049 --> 00:17:51.039
and well that’s a little bit more optimistic
representation of the tyranny of social order

00:17:51.039 --> 00:17:56.140
that pure compulsion right because what it
suggests is there's a way that you can organize

00:17:56.140 --> 00:18:03.149
the way you are genuinely and the way I am
genuinely so that we both are genuine yet

00:18:03.149 --> 00:18:08.270
we get something more than we would get if
